window.GUIDES = {"algebraic/bipartite-matching": {"title": "Algebraic Bipartite Matching", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>You want to know whether a bipartite graph has a <em>perfect matching</em> — without running an augmenting-path algorithm. The algebraic idea: build a matrix whose <b>determinant is a polynomial that is nonzero exactly when a matching exists</b>. Each permutation term in the determinant expansion corresponds to a way of pairing the two sides; a surviving term is a perfect matching. Then test \"is this polynomial nonzero?\" with PIT and Schwartz–Zippel.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Does this bipartite graph have a perfect matching?\" &rarr; symbolic determinant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Need a fast randomized check (or a parallel one) instead of combinatorial augmenting paths.</li>\n<li>Counting flavor: \"parity of the number of perfect matchings\" &rarr; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x1D53D;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">SYMBOLIC (TUTTE) MATRIX</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nFor bipartite <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, define <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> with a <em>distinct variable</em> on each edge:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>if&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2pt</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd columnalign=\"left\"><mtext>otherwise.</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">encoding a bipartite graph so that matchings become determinant terms</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">DET ↔ MATCHING</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>sgn</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nA term survives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math> is a perfect matching. Because each variable is distinct, no two terms cancel, so\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02262;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><mi>G</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;has&#x000A0;a&#x000A0;perfect&#x000A0;matching.</mtext></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">turning \"matching exists?\" into \"polynomial nonzero?\"</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">TEST BY PIT</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> but exponentially many monomials — don't expand it. Substitute random values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02190;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and compute the <em>numeric</em> determinant in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Nonzero <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> matching exists; error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> by Schwartz–Zippel, amplify by repetition.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">deciding the symbolic determinant is nonzero, efficiently</span></div></div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>2024C P1d.</b> True/False: the <em>parity</em> of the number of perfect matchings in a bipartite graph is efficiently decidable via <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Set all variables to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> biadjacency matrix (each present edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>). Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></msub><mo>sgn</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> sums <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> over all perfect matchings.</li>\n<li><b>Reduce mod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</b> Over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x1D53D;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> the signs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>sgn</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> collapse to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>perfect&#x000A0;matchings</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>It is efficient.</b> A determinant mod <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> is computable by Gaussian elimination over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x1D53D;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — deterministic, no randomness needed. So the statement is <b>True</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Contrast.</b> This gives only the <em>parity</em>. Counting matchings exactly is #P-hard (permanent), and <em>existence</em> needs the symbolic determinant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></math> PIT above, not the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> determinant.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> determinant is <em>not</em> a matching test: signed terms can cancel, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> does not mean \"no matching\" (e.g. the all-ones <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrix has a matching yet <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). That cancellation is exactly why you need <em>distinct variables</em> — they make the terms unable to cancel. One direction is easy without algebra (a nonzero numeric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> matching), but the clean equivalence needs the symbolic matrix. And keep <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> so the Schwartz–Zippel error stays <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>; otherwise no perfect matching (matrix isn't square).</li>\n<li>Build the symbolic matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> with a distinct variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> per edge.</li>\n<li>Argue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02262;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> perfect matching exists (surviving permutation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> matching, no cancellation).</li>\n<li>Test <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02262;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> by PIT: random substitution, numeric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>State the one-sided error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>; amplify. (For parity only, use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> — deterministic.)</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2024C-p1"]}, "algebraic/fft": {"title": "FFT, Convolution & Karatsuba", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>Multiplying two degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> polynomials the naïve way costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: every coefficient of the product is a <em>convolution</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. The FFT does it in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The trick: a polynomial has two faces — its list of coefficients, and its list of <em>values</em> at chosen points. Multiplication is slow in coefficients but trivial in values (multiply pointwise). The FFT is the fast bridge between the two faces, using the roots of unity as the evaluation points.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Any convolution / \"for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, count pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>\" (sumsets, string matching) &rarr; FFT.</li>\n<li>Multiply two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-bit integers or two polynomials &rarr; FFT (or Karatsuba for a simpler <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li>Divide polynomials, evaluate at many points &rarr; built on FFT.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">EVALUATE → MULTIPLY → INTERPOLATE</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nTo compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>: (1) evaluate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> at the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> roots of unity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>; (2) multiply values pointwise (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> mults); (3) interpolate back. A degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> polynomial is determined by its values at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> distinct points, so this is exact.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">you need a product / convolution and only have coefficients</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">FFT (evaluation at roots of unity)</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nWith <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> a power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, split <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>even</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>odd</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>even</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>odd</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>even</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mtext>odd</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The squares <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> are exactly the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> roots of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so recurse:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">converting coefficients ↔ values at the roots of unity</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">INVERSE FFT</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nThe evaluation map is the Vandermonde/DFT matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Its inverse is almost itself: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, i.e. run the same FFT with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and divide by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. So interpolation is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">going from product values back to coefficients</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">KARATSUBA</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nSplit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> products use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, recovering the cross term as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>.\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1.585</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">a simpler sub-quadratic multiply, no complex numbers needed</span></div></div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>2023A P6.</b> Given sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, compute the sumset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> and, for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>, the number of ways to write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Encode sets as polynomials.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></math> (indicator coefficients).</li>\n<li><b>Read the answer off the product.</b> In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>s</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow></math>, the coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>:</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> — exactly the representation count. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is the set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Multiply with FFT.</b> Both polynomials have degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so the product has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>: evaluate at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> roots of unity, multiply pointwise, inverse-FFT. Total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Extract.</b> Scan the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math>: positive entries give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>; the values are the counts.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> Pad both inputs to a power of two that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — if the transform length is too short the product <em>wraps around</em> (cyclic convolution) and you get wrong coefficients. FFT's <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> is a root of unity; it has <em>nothing</em> to do with the matrix-multiplication exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.37</mn></mrow></math> (same letter, unrelated). Interpolation is <em>not</em> the transpose of evaluation — it needs the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> factor and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Phrase the task as a convolution / polynomial product.</li>\n<li>Set the transform length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, pad with zeros.</li>\n<li>FFT both, multiply pointwise, inverse-FFT (divide by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li>Read the answer off the product coefficients.</li>\n<li>Total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; for integers, split into digit-polynomials first.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2023A-p6"]}, "algebraic/matrix-mult": {"title": "Matrix-Multiplication Applications", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>Fast matrix multiplication is a black box: two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrices multiply in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2.373</mn></mrow></math> (naïvely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>). The exam skill is <em>reduction</em>: recast a graph or counting problem as a matrix product (or a matrix power) so you beat <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Powers of the adjacency matrix count walks; repeated squaring computes high powers cheaply; and randomness lets you <em>verify</em> a product faster than you can compute it.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Count walks / triangles / reachability\" &rarr; powers of the adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>\"Transitive closure / diameter in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" &rarr; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> via repeated squaring + search.</li>\n<li>\"Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>\" &rarr; stack the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> into a matrix, one product (economy of scale).</li>\n<li>\"Check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> fast\" &rarr; Freivalds' randomized verifier in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">STRASSEN / EXPONENT ω</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nStrassen multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> blocks with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> multiplications instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2.81</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nOnly the number of multiplications matters: multiplying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> mults gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. Known: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2.373</mn></mrow></math>.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">any dense product; a subroutine you invoke as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math></span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">WALK COUNTING</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nFor adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mtext>walks&#x000A0;of&#x000A0;length&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>k</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;from&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;to&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> some walk of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> exists; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>tr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi></mrow></math>triangles.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">reachability, path existence, counting closed walks</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">BINARY EXPONENTIATION</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nCompute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> products: square repeatedly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, then multiply the factors picked out by the binary digits of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">you need a single high power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> (or a search over powers)</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">FREIVALDS' VERIFIER</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nTo test <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: pick random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>B</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> using three matrix–vector products. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> always passes; if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is nonzero, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> fools it with prob. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> (Schwartz–Zippel, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>).\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">verify a claimed product faster than recomputing it</span></div></div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>2024B P4.</b> Compute the <em>diameter</em> of a strongly connected digraph in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (deterministically).</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Turn reachability into a matrix power.</b> Use the lazy walk matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> there is a walk from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> — the \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>\" lets shorter paths persist as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> grows.</li>\n<li><b>Distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> smallest good power.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; the diameter is the least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> for which <em>all</em> entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> are positive.</li>\n<li><b>Monotonicity → binary search.</b> \"All entries positive\" only becomes truer as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> grows, so binary-search <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Cost.</b> Each tested power is one binary exponentiation, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> search steps that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. (Precomputing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>0</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math> once shaves a log.)</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> <b>Linear-algebra facts you can quote.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>rank</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>rank</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (equal kernels). <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is PSD <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> all eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> for some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>tr</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. A symmetric matrix with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> need <em>not</em> be PSD (e.g. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> in even dimension).</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> Reducing to matrix multiplication only beats <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> — always cite the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> black box, don't silently assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2.37</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is free of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> factors from squaring/search. Boolean product: cast <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> entries to integers, multiply, then threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> — over the booleans there is no fast algorithm directly. Diameter/APSP tricks need the graph connected (or handle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math> distances). Repeated squaring gives a <em>single</em> power in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — computing all powers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> still costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> products.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Express the quantity as a product, a power, or \"smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> positive\".</li>\n<li>Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> if you want \"length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>\" instead of \"exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>\".</li>\n<li>Use binary exponentiation for high powers; binary-search the threshold if it is monotone.</li>\n<li>Count products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; add the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> factors honestly and confirm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>To only <em>verify</em> a product, use Freivalds in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> instead of recomputing.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2024B-p4"]}, "algebraic/pit": {"title": "PIT & Schwartz–Zippel", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>You have a polynomial that is <em>huge</em> to write out (a determinant, a product of many factors, a formula) and you only need to know one thing: <b>is it the zero polynomial or not?</b> You cannot afford to expand it — but you <em>can</em> plug in numbers. That is exactly Polynomial Identity Testing (PIT): test <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> by evaluating <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> at a random point.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Are two expressions/circuits/matrices equal?\" &rarr; test their difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is the zero polynomial.</li>\n<li>\"Does this graph have a perfect matching / a subspace contain an invertible matrix?\" &rarr; a determinant is a nonzero polynomial exactly when the object exists.</li>\n<li>The polynomial has low degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> but exponentially many monomials &rarr; evaluate, don't expand.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">SCHWARTZ–ZIPPEL</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nLet <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> be a polynomial in any number of variables of total degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, over a field, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> be a finite set. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> chosen independently and uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">a nonzero low-degree polynomial rarely vanishes at a random point</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">PIT ALGORITHM</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nTo decide <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, draw a random point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>, evaluate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Output \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\" iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. One-sided error: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> always correct; if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> wrong with prob. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Repeat <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> times &rarr; error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">the polynomial is expensive to expand but cheap to evaluate at a point</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> <b>Univariate special case.</b> A nonzero single-variable polynomial of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> roots. So over any set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> distinct points it is nonzero at one of them. Schwartz–Zippel is the multivariate generalization (proved by induction on the number of variables — this is a homework exercise).</div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>2024A P6.</b> Given matrices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>, decide whether their span contains an <em>invertible</em> matrix.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Build one polynomial that \"sees\" the whole span.</b> A generic element of the span is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Its determinant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math> is a polynomial in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Translate the question into \"is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> the zero polynomial?\"</b> The span contains an invertible matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> some assignment makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02262;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Bound the degree.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo></mrow></math> of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix is a sum over permutations of products of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> entries, each entry linear in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Run PIT.</b> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, plug in random integers, compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by Gaussian elimination in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. If nonzero, answer YES; error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, amplify by repetition.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> Schwartz–Zippel bounds probability by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so you must choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> (or larger) — a small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> can make the bound useless or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The error is <em>one-sided</em>: \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\" is only <em>evidence</em> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, never a proof. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> is the <em>total</em> degree (sum of exponents), not the number of variables. And note: a nonzero degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> polynomial can still vanish on a huge set (e.g. all order-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> roots of unity if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>) — SZ only helps when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Identify the polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> whose (non)vanishing answers the question — usually a determinant or a difference <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Bound its total degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> (for a determinant, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li>Choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>; sample a random point.</li>\n<li>Evaluate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> at the point efficiently (never expand it).</li>\n<li>State the one-sided error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and amplify by repetition to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2024A-p6"]}, "approx/approximation": {"title": "Approximation Algorithms", "html": "\n<h3>What you are actually being asked to do</h3>\n<p>An approximation algorithm returns a <i>legal</i> solution fast, together with a <i>proof</i> of how far it can be from the optimum. You never know OPT — that is the whole point (the problem is NP-hard). So the entire game is:</p>\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">The one definition</span> For a <b>minimization</b> problem, ALG is an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math>-approximation (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) if on every input <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> For a <b>maximization</b> problem (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) it is <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> You do <i>not</i> need ALG to contain an optimal solution — only to be provably close in value.</div>\n\n<p>Because OPT is unknown, you can never compare to it directly. Instead you sandwich it between two quantities you <i>can</i> compute or reason about. This is the master pattern that every problem below is a special case of.</p>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">Pattern 1: Lower-bound OPT, upper-bound ALG</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">Find a <b>quantity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math></b> (matching size, LP value, sum of weights, an edge count) that you can control on both sides. Show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math> (min) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math>. Chaining gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>. For maximization, flip: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>.<span class=\"gtool-use\">every approximation proof — pick the right bridge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math></span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">Pattern 2: Greedy / maximal</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">Build the solution by a simple rule (pick an uncovered edge, the min-degree vertex, sets in ratio order) until nothing is left. The lower bound almost always comes from a <b>disjointness / packing</b> argument: the objects you greedily grabbed are pairwise \"independent,\" so OPT must pay for each separately.<span class=\"gtool-use\">vertex cover, hitting set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, independent set on max-degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math> graphs</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">Pattern 3: LP-rounding (IP &rarr; relax &rarr; round)</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">Three mechanical steps: (i) write the problem as a 0/1 <b>integer program</b>; (ii) <b>relax</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and solve the LP in poly-time — this gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mtext>OPT</mtext><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>; (iii) <b>round</b> the fractional <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> to a legal integral solution and bound how much rounding costs.<span class=\"gtool-use\">any problem with a natural 0/1 IP: set cover, hitting set, knapsack</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">Pattern 4: Local / combinatorial structure</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">Exploit a graph object you can compute exactly: a <b>matching</b>, an <b>MST</b>, a <b>minimum cycle cover</b>, or a <b>primal-dual</b> pair. These give clean lower bounds — e.g. a metric TSP tour contains a Hamiltonian cycle whose two perfect matchings each cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.<span class=\"gtool-use\">TSP / Christofides, TSP with weights 1&ndash;2, Steiner tree, primal-dual set cover</span></div></div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the maximal pattern in full</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\"><p><b>2014A P9.</b> Prove any <i>maximal</i> matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> (one you cannot add another edge to) is a 2-approximation for <i>maximum</i> matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Trivial upper bound.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> is a matching, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math> — never worse than optimal.</li>\n<li><b>Lower bound via disjointness.</b> Every edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> must touch a vertex saturated by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>; otherwise that edge could be added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>, contradicting maximality.</li>\n<li><b>Count.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> saturates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> vertices, and each is an endpoint of at most one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math>-edge (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is a matching). So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Chain.</b> <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div> a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for the (maximization) matching problem.</li>\n<li><b>Free corollary — Vertex Cover 2-approx.</b> Output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, both endpoints of every matched edge. It is a cover (any uncovered edge would extend <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>), the matched edges are disjoint so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mtext>OPT</mtext><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, and <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mtext>OPT</mtext><mrow><mi>V</mi><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> Same maximal object, mirrored as a min problem.</li>\n</ol></div>\n\n<h3>The problems, mapped to patterns</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Greedy/maximal:</b> hitting set with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> &rarr; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-approx by disjoint sets (<b>2018A P2</b>, hypergraph VC <b>2023B P3</b>); min-degree greedy independent set &rarr; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-approx (<b>2018B P2</b>, <b>hw2025 s5 P2</b>).</li>\n<li><b>LP-rounding:</b> hitting set — since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>; round up those, cost grows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo></mrow></math> &rarr; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-approx (<b>2024B P5</b>). Knapsack as BIP/LP/dual (<b>2017B P2</b>). Randomized rounding for set cover: each set with prob <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msubsup></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mtext>OPT</mtext><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>, each element covered w.p. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>, repeat <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> rounds &rarr; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-approx by union bound (<b>hw2026 s4 P3</b>).</li>\n<li><b>Random assignment / probabilistic:</b> MAX-3SAT — for each clause let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator r.v. for the event \"clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> is satisfied\"; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, so by linearity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>, then derandomize by conditional expectations (<b>2014A P8</b>). Max Triple Cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (<b>2023A P3</b>).</li>\n<li><b>Combinatorial:</b> TSP min-weight matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> (<b>2015B P3</b>); Christofides path-TSP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math> (<b>2017A P3</b>); weights-1&ndash;2 TSP via min cycle cover &rarr; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (<b>2021A P3</b>); Steiner reduction (<b>2014A P5</b>).</li>\n<li><b>Primal-dual:</b> set cover — solve the dual, pick sets with tight constraints; complementary slackness gives an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approx (<b>2025C P2</b>).</li>\n<li><b>Greedy/local, other:</b> bin packing additive-1 for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> weights (<b>2015A P6</b>); greedy MAX-3-COL <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (<b>2021B P3</b>).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> (1) A greedy ratio must be <i>proven</i>, not assumed — \"sort by ratio\" fails on knapsack and max cut, and you are often asked to <b>refute</b> a claimed ratio with a counterexample (<b>2016A P5</b>, <b>2017B P2a</b>, <b>2018B P2a</b>). (2) Rounding <i>up</i> is safe for min-covering constraints but can violate a knapsack <i>capacity</i> — always re-check feasibility after rounding. (3) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mtext>OPT</mtext><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math> only for a <i>minimization</i> IP; the inequality flips for maximization. (4) Metric/triangle-inequality assumptions are what make MST- and matching-based TSP bounds valid — state them.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist for ANY approximation question</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Min or max?</b> Fix the target direction of the inequality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math> (or the flip).</li>\n<li><b>Legality first.</b> Prove ALG always outputs a feasible solution before touching the ratio.</li>\n<li><b>Choose the bridge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Matching / LP value / packing count / MST — the thing you can bound against OPT.</li>\n<li><b>Lower-bound OPT.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math> (min) via disjointness or LP relaxation; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math> for the max side.</li>\n<li><b>Upper-bound ALG.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math> — count what the algorithm pays.</li>\n<li><b>Chain and read off <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>.</b> State the ratio and confirm the running time is polynomial.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2014A-p9"]}, "lp/basics": {"title": "LP Basics & Geometry", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>Any question that says \"linear program\", \"minimize/maximize a linear objective subject to linear constraints\", \"put in standard form\", \"run one simplex pivot\", or \"is this point a vertex\". The whole game is: an LP lives on a polyhedron, and its optimum sits at a corner. So we learn to (a) write the LP cleanly, (b) recognize corners, (c) walk between them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Convert this LP\" / \"add slack variables\" &rarr; rewrite into <b>standard form</b>.</li>\n<li>\"Is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> a vertex / basic feasible solution?\" &rarr; the <b>independent-columns</b> test.</li>\n<li>\"Which variable enters / leaves the basis?\" &rarr; one <b>simplex pivot</b> (reduced cost, then min-ratio).</li>\n<li>\"Prove LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02208;</mo></mrow></math> P\" &rarr; cite the <b>ellipsoid</b> algorithm, never simplex.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">STANDARD FORM</div><div class=\"gtool-body\"><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math></div> To get here: flip <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></math> by negating <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>; turn <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (a <b>slack</b>); split a free variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"put into standard form\", \"add slack variables\"</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">VERTEX = BASIC FEASIBLE SOLUTION</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">For feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;a&#x000A0;vertex</mtext><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;has&#x000A0;independent&#x000A0;columns.</mtext></mrow></math></div> Equivalently <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex iff there is no <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>. A standard-form polytope in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> vertices. <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> a vertex / BFS?\"</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">SIMPLEX (one pivot)</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">With basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, reduced costs are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>. <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>current&#x000A0;BFS&#x000A0;optimal.</mtext></mrow></math></div> Otherwise raise a variable with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; the <b>min-ratio test</b> says how far. <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"which variable enters/leaves?\"</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">ELLIPSOID</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">Trap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> in a big ball, repeatedly halve the volume around a separating hyperplane until you hit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> or prove it empty. Runs in <b>polynomial time</b> &mdash; this is the theorem \"LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02208;</mo></mrow></math> P\". Slow in practice; only needs a <b>separation oracle</b>, so it even handles exponentially many constraints. <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"prove LP is solvable in poly time\"</span></div></div>\n\n<h3>Key facts</h3>\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is finite over a standard-form polytope, the optimum is attained at a <b>vertex</b>. Intuition: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is not a corner, some direction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math>; slide along the non-worsening side until a coordinate hits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> &mdash; you gain a zero coordinate each time, so after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps you reach a corner no worse than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</div>\n<div class=\"gdef\"><span class=\"lab\">Definition</span> A <b>basis</b> is a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> column indices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> invertible; its BFS sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and is feasible when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. \"Degenerate\" means some basic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</div>\n\n<h3>Worked example &mdash; the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\"><p><b>2014A P10.</b> An LP with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> constraints; find a BFS, then do one pivot.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Constraints are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>, so I add one nonneg slack per row to reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The slacks hand me a free starting BFS: set the original <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, each slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Asked for the BFS of basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: keep those two basic, force every other variable to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and solve the square system <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> for the two values.</li>\n<li>Entering variable: compute reduced costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>; pick a nonbasic column with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (most negative is the standard greedy choice). If none is negative, stop &mdash; already optimal.</li>\n<li>Leaving variable: the min-ratio test. Over rows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> where the entering column is positive, take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mtext>column&#x000A0;entry</mtext><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; the smallest ratio names who leaves. This is exactly \"raise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> until the first basic variable hits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\", keeping <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Swap them in the basis, recompute the objective at the new vertex.</li>\n</ol></div>\n<p>The theory behind step 3 is <b>2025B P3</b>: prove <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> optimal. Reason: any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n\n<h3>Common traps</h3>\n<div class=\"box pitfall\">A slack must be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and you never add a slack to an equality. &mdash; A <b>free</b> variable is NOT just \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\"; you must split it <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. &mdash; <b>Degeneracy</b> (2023A P1): a pivot can change the basis yet stay at the same physical vertex, so the objective need not strictly improve &mdash; but a move to a genuinely new vertex does strictly improve. &mdash; Never claim simplex is polynomial: a single pivot is poly, the number of pivots is exponential worst-case. The poly-time result is <b>ellipsoid</b>. &mdash; Deciding \"is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> a vertex?\" is easy (rank check), a recurring T/F (2024B P1).</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist &mdash; one simplex pivot</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Standard form: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>min</mo></mrow></math>, add nonneg slacks, split free vars, enforce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Fix the current basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>; set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>; if all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> &rarr; STOP (optimal).</li>\n<li>Entering <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> min-ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> over positive entries.</li>\n<li>Update basis, recompute the objective.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2014A-p10"]}, "lp/duality-farkas": {"title": "LP Duality & Farkas", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>Whenever a question says \"write the dual\", \"prove the optimum is at least / at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>\", \"show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is optimal without solving\", or hands you an infeasible/unbounded LP. The one idea underneath everything: the dual is the <b>best certificate</b> for a bound on the primal. Every dual-feasible point is a proof; strong duality says the best proof is tight.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Write the dual\" &rarr; the mechanical recipe below.</li>\n<li>\"Prove OPT <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>\" (a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></math>) &rarr; exhibit ONE feasible <b>dual</b> point of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>; weak duality does the rest.</li>\n<li>\"Show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> is optimal\" &rarr; build a matching dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup></mrow></math> with equal value, or check <b>complementary slackness</b>.</li>\n<li>\"(P) infeasible, (D) feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> ?\" &rarr; (D) is <b>unbounded</b>, via <b>Farkas</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">PRIMAL / DUAL PAIR</div><div class=\"gtool-body\"><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>(P)</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>(D)</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math></div> The dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is free (one per equality constraint). The dual of the dual is the primal. <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"write the dual\"</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">WEAK / STRONG DUALITY</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">Weak: for all feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>since&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> Strong: if BOTH (P) and (D) are feasible then their optima are <b>equal</b>. <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"prove a bound\", \"reduce optimization to feasibility\"</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">FARKAS' LEMMA</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">Exactly one holds: <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>OR</mtext><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>y</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a certificate of infeasibility. Variant II: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> unsolvable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. This is the engine that proves strong duality. <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"prove infeasible\", \"prove unbounded\"</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">COMPLEMENTARY SLACKNESS</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">At a joint optimum, for each variable/constraint pair: <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> \"Money changes hands only where the constraint is tight.\" <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"verify optimality of a given pair\"</span></div></div>\n\n<h3>The dual, mechanically (memorize)</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Count.</b> One dual variable per primal <b>constraint</b>; one dual constraint per primal <b>variable</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Flip the sense.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo>&#x02194;</mo><mo>max</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Swap data.</b> Primal RHS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> becomes the dual objective; primal cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> becomes the dual RHS; use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup></mrow></math> for the constraint matrix.</li>\n<li><b>Variable signs from primal constraint type</b> (for a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></math> primal): primal \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math>\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> dual var free; primal \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> dual var <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; primal \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> dual var <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Constraint sense from primal variable type:</b> primal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> dual constraint is \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\"; primal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> free <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> dual constraint is \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math>\".</li>\n<li><b>Sanity check</b> with weak duality: dual value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> primal value at any feasible pair.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h3>Worked example &mdash; the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\"><p><b>2015A P7.</b> \"Write the dual, then prove the dual optimum is at least 40.\" I never solve the LP.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Write the dual by the recipe above (transpose, swap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02194;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, flip <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></math>/<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li>To bound the optimum, I only need ONE feasible point on the right side and read off its value; weak duality turns that single point into a proof of the bound. Producing a matching value on each side pins OPT exactly (strong duality).</li>\n</ol></div>\n<p>The recurring exam pattern is <b>\"write the IP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> relax to LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> dualize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> interpret\"</b>. In <b>2023A P4</b> and <b>2014B P8</b> you relax maximum matching, transpose, and the dual is exactly <b>vertex cover</b> &mdash; K&ouml;nig's theorem falls out. <b>2017A P6</b> does the same for hitting set, whose dual is set packing. <b>2025A P2</b> is a self-dual symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>: show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is optimal by matching primal and dual values. <b>2024C P6</b> is the pure Farkas move: (P) infeasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></math> (D) feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> (D) unbounded &mdash; Farkas hands a direction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> along which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n\n<h3>Common traps</h3>\n<div class=\"box pitfall\">The dual of a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo></mrow></math> is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></math> &mdash; getting the direction backwards flips everything. &mdash; Wrong <b>variable sign</b>: equality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> free variable, inequality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> signed variable; a mismatch flips <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> in the dual constraints. &mdash; Strong duality needs BOTH sides feasible; if one is infeasible you get the unbounded/infeasible relation, not equal optima (2023B P1 T/F). &mdash; Don't forget to <b>transpose</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and to swap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. &mdash; Complementary slackness is an \"iff at optimum\" tool, not something you can assume mid-solve.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist &mdash; write the dual</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Count: dual vars <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> #primal constraints, dual constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> #primal vars.</li>\n<li>Swap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo>&#x02194;</mo><mo>max</mo></mrow></math>; move <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> into the objective, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> into the RHS; use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Set each dual <b>variable</b>'s sign from its primal constraint type.</li>\n<li>Set each dual <b>constraint</b>'s sense from its primal variable's sign.</li>\n<li>Verify with weak duality: dual value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> primal value.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2015A-p7"]}, "lp/ilp-tum": {"title": "Integer Programming & TUM", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>Every exam has a \"write an integer program for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>\" question, then follow-ups: relax it, and ask whether the relaxation is integral. The mental model: an IP is an LP plus \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> integer\", which is NP-hard; the LP relaxation is a poly-time <b>lower bound</b> (for min) that is sometimes exactly right &mdash; and TUM is the property that tells you when.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Model <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> as an IP / BIP\" &rarr; binary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> plus coverage / capacity / conflict constraints.</li>\n<li>\"Write the LP relaxation\" &rarr; replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (drop integrality).</li>\n<li>\"Is the relaxation integral / what is the integrality gap?\" &rarr; ask whether the matrix is <b>TUM</b>; if not, find a fractional optimum.</li>\n<li>\"Why not just solve the IP directly?\" &rarr; ILP is NP-hard; a poly-time solver <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> P <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> NP.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">IP BUILDING BLOCKS</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\"cover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; \"not both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>\" (conflict) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; \"exactly one bin\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; \"capacity\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>ILP</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>LP</mtext><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x02124;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>NP-hard.</mtext></mrow></math></div> <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"model X as an IP/BIP\"</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">LP RELAXATION &amp; INTEGRALITY GAP</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">Relax <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. For a minimization, <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>LP-OPT</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>IP-OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>gap</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>IP-OPT</mtext></mrow><mrow><mtext>LP-OPT</mtext></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> The relaxation is a poly-time bound; the gap measures how much you lose. <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"write the relaxation\", \"give the integrality gap\"</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">TOTAL UNIMODULARITY (TUM)</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">Integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is TUM if every square submatrix has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;TUM</mtext><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>b</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;integral</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>every&#x000A0;vertex&#x000A0;of&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;is&#x000A0;integral.</mtext></mrow></math></div> Then the LP relaxation already returns an integer optimum, so it solves the IP in poly time. <span class=\"gtool-use\">\"is the relaxation exact?\"</span></div></div>\n\n<h3>Why TUM works, and the classic cases</h3>\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> A vertex has a basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. By Cramer's rule each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a ratio of determinants; if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is TUM then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> is integral, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> is an integer vector. Hence all vertices are integral.</div>\n<div class=\"gdef\"><span class=\"lab\">Definition</span> Bipartite-matching and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> flow constraint matrices are TUM. Consequences you should recognize on sight: fractional bipartite matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> integral matching; Max-Flow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> Min-Cut; Max Bipartite Matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> Min Vertex Cover (K&ouml;nig). General vertex cover, dominating set, set cover, independent set are <b>not</b> TUM.</div>\n\n<h3>Worked example &mdash; the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\"><p><b>2014A P4.</b> \"Write minimum-weight dominating set as an ILP, then give an instance where the LP relaxation is fractional.\"</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Variables: one binary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> per vertex (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> in the set). Objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Domination constraint: every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> must be covered by itself or a neighbor, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Relax: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Fractional witness: on a symmetric graph (a triangle / small clique) the LP can set every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; each covering constraint sums to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and the LP pays less than any integral solution. That difference is the <b>integrality gap</b>, and it exists precisely because this matrix is NOT TUM.</li>\n</ol></div>\n<p>Same covering shape recurs in <b>2025B P2</b> (weighted set cover): \"formulate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> relax <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo></mrow></math> round\" gives an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation by threshold-rounding at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> (take every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>); each element of frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> is still covered, and cost grows by at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>. <b>2024A P4</b> (Max-SAT), <b>2024C P4</b> (bin packing), <b>2023A P2</b> (dominating set) are pure modeling. <b>2024B P1</b> is the complexity T/F: a poly-time ILP solver would settle independent set, so P <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> NP.</p>\n\n<h3>Common traps</h3>\n<div class=\"box pitfall\">Do not assume the relaxation is always integral &mdash; it is guaranteed only when the matrix is <b>TUM</b> (bipartite matching yes; dominating set / general vertex cover / set cover no). &mdash; \"All entries are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" does NOT imply TUM; you need every square-submatrix determinant in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. &mdash; Rounding a fractional LP solution can break feasibility; naive thresholds do not always give a feasible integral point (2021A/2021B) &mdash; justify why your rounding stays feasible. &mdash; Do not drop the objective sense or forget that IP variables are integers, not merely bounded.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist &mdash; IP + relaxation</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Pick variables (usually binary <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> for assignments).</li>\n<li>Write objective and constraints from the building blocks (cover / capacity / conflict); justify each.</li>\n<li>Relax: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Is the matrix TUM with integral <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>? If yes &rarr; relaxation is exact, poly-time.</li>\n<li>If not TUM, expect an integrality gap: find a symmetric all-halves fractional witness, or round for an approximation and prove it stays feasible.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2014A-p4"]}, "randomized/concentration": {"title": "Concentration — Markov, Chebyshev, Chernoff", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>A random quantity has a known mean, and you want to say it is <em>close</em> to that mean — or that a bad deviation is rare. Pick the tool by how much you know and how sharp a bound you need.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Only the mean, variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> &rarr; <b>Markov</b> (weak, but always available).</li>\n<li>Mean + variance &rarr; <b>Chebyshev</b> (two-sided, polynomial tail).</li>\n<li>A <em>sum of independent</em> 0/1 (or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) variables &rarr; <b>Chernoff</b> (exponential tail — the workhorse).</li>\n<li>\"How many samples to estimate a fraction within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>?\" &rarr; Chernoff, solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>\"Show a good object exists w.h.p.\" &rarr; Chernoff on one condition, then union bound over all conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">MARKOV</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nFor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">only the mean known; also the engine behind Las Vegas &rarr; Monte Carlo</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">CHEBYSHEV</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">you can compute the variance and want a two-sided bound</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">CHERNOFF (0/1 sum)</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nFor independent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">a sum of many independent indicators concentrates sharply</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">CHERNOFF (symmetric <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>)</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nFor independent uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mstyle></mrow></math>.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">random signs / colorings: discrepancy and balancing arguments</span></div></div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>2024A P2.</b> Estimate a CNF formula's satisfying-assignment density to within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Turn \"estimate\" into a sample mean.</b> Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> uniform assignments; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> indicate \"sample <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> satisfies\". The estimator is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mover><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> (the true density).</li>\n<li><b>Pick Chernoff.</b> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent indicators — exactly its setting. We want <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mover><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</b> Set the bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and solve: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> samples suffice. The failure probability shrinks <em>exponentially</em> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, which is why a poly number of samples buys <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>State independence.</b> The bound is only valid because the samples are drawn independently — say it explicitly.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> <b>Chernoff + union bound pattern.</b> To show a random object is good with high probability: (1) fix <em>one</em> condition (a set, a pair), bound its failure by Chernoff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi></mrow></math>; (2) union-bound over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> conditions; (3) if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, a good object <em>must exist</em>. Multipliers: all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math>; all subsets/colorings <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> Chernoff needs <em>independence</em> — check it. Match the variable type to the right bound (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> vs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, one- vs two-sided). Markov alone is far too weak for sampling — it will not give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. When union-bounding, count the conditions correctly (subsets? pairs of subsets? <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>).</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Identify the random sum and its mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Choose the tool by what you know: mean (Markov), + variance (Chebyshev), independent sum (Chernoff).</li>\n<li>For sample counts: set the tail <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> target failure, solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>For existence: Chernoff on one condition, then union bound; show total failure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Confirm and state independence.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2024A-p2"]}, "randomized/isolation-lemma": {"title": "The Isolation Lemma — Making the Optimum Unique", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>You have a huge family of combinatorial objects (paths, matchings, sets) and an algebraic tool that only works when the <em>best</em> one is <em>unique</em> — ties would cancel or confuse it. Random weights break all ties at once: with good probability exactly one object has minimum weight.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Make the min-weight path / matching unique\" &rarr; assign random weights, apply the lemma.</li>\n<li>Symbolic-determinant / matrix methods that fail on ties &rarr; isolate first, then evaluate.</li>\n<li>\"Unique min-weight object <em>for every</em> pair simultaneously\" &rarr; lemma per pair + union bound.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tool</h3>\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> <b>Isolation Lemma.</b> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> be any family of subsets of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Give each element an independent uniform weight in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Then\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>a&#x000A0;unique&#x000A0;set&#x000A0;in&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;has&#x000A0;minimum&#x000A0;weight</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe bound depends only on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> — <em>not</em> on how many sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> contains (which may be exponential).</div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">TIE-BREAK BY RANDOM WEIGHTS</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nTake <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (e.g. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> gives failure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>). Weights stay small, so the algebra stays polynomial-size.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">whenever an algebraic method needs a unique optimum</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gdef\"><span class=\"lab\">Definition</span> <b>Why an element is \"singular\".</b> Element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is undecided if the best set containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and the best set avoiding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> tie. Fixing all other weights, exactly one weight value for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> causes a tie, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;singular</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. Union bound over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> elements: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>any&#x000A0;tie</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. No tie <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> unique minimum.</div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>2024A P3.</b> Show random edge weights in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> give a unique minimum-weight path for <em>every</em> vertex pair with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>One pair first.</b> Fix vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The family <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> paths (viewed as edge subsets). The lemma gives a unique min-weight path with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>.</b> There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> pairs. Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> large enough that each failure is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> — polynomial weights suffice.</li>\n<li><b>Union bound over pairs.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>some&#x000A0;pair&#x000A0;fails</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>, so all pairs are isolated with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Payoff.</b> With every optimum unique, downstream algebra (determinant / matrix powers) reads off the answer without ties cancelling.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> <b>Weights into a determinant.</b> To find a min-weight object via a symbolic determinant, encode weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>: a unique minimum makes the lowest power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> survive without cancellation, so the determinant's low-order behaviour reveals the optimum (e.g. min-weight perfect matching with exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> red edges, hw2025s2 P3).</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> The lemma isolates <em>one</em> family; for many pairs you must union-bound (which costs a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> factor in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>). The bound is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>, independent of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x02131;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> — do not try to sum over the exponentially many sets. Keep <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> polynomial so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> encodings and determinants stay poly-size.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Identify the family <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> of objects (as subsets of a ground set).</li>\n<li>Assign independent uniform weights in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Apply the lemma: unique minimum with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Need it for many families? Union bound; size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> accordingly.</li>\n<li>Feed the now-unique optimum into the algebraic method (e.g. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> in a determinant).</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2024A-p3"]}, "randomized/karger-min-cut": {"title": "Karger's Contraction — Surviving the Min-Cut", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>Any question about the <em>global</em> minimum cut of an undirected graph, or that asks you to <em>count</em> min-cuts. The whole method is one idea: if you keep merging random edges, a <em>fixed</em> min-cut has a decent chance of never being merged — and \"decent chance × many repetitions = high probability\".</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Find / approximate the min-cut\" &rarr; run contraction, repeat, take the best.</li>\n<li>\"Prove a graph has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> min-cuts\" &rarr; use the survival lower bound as a <em>counting</em> argument.</li>\n<li>Anything where a random merge might destroy a special structure &rarr; bound the per-step survival, multiply.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The algorithm</h3>\n<div class=\"gdef\"><span class=\"lab\">Definition</span> <b>Karger contraction:</b> while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, pick a uniformly random edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, merge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> into one vertex, and delete self-loops (keep parallel edges). Output the cut given by the two surviving super-vertices.</div>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">DEGREE / EDGE-COUNT BOUND</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nIf the min-cut has size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, so\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">to lower-bound how many edges are still around, hence upper-bound the chance of hitting a cut edge</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">PER-STEP SURVIVAL</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nFix one min-cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. In iteration <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> (with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vertices left),\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>e</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;crosses&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">the event you must AVOID at every single step</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> A fixed min-cut survives all contractions with probability\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>survives</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nRunning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> independent trials and keeping the best cut makes the failure probability tiny.</div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>2024B P3.</b> Prove a graph has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> minimum cuts.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Reframe counting as probability.</b> Label the distinct min-cuts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math>. One run outputs <em>exactly one</em> cut, so the events \"run outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>\" are disjoint.</li>\n<li><b>Lower-bound each.</b> Outputting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> to survive, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>output&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> — the same bound for every min-cut.</li>\n<li><b>Sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</b> Disjoint events: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>output&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Solve.</b> Rearranging gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The probability bound became a <em>counting</em> bound — no algorithm needs to run.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> Delete self-loops but <em>keep parallel edges</em> — they carry the multiplicity that makes edge sampling correct. The survival bound is for <em>one fixed</em> cut chosen before the run, not \"some cut\". Never assume the min-cut is unique. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> bound uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, which needs the min-cut of the <em>contracted</em> graph to still be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Fix one target min-cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> up front.</li>\n<li>Per step: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>hit&#x000A0;a&#x000A0;cut&#x000A0;edge</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>vertices&#x000A0;left</mtext></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Multiply the survival factors &rarr; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>To count cuts: disjoint outputs, sum probabilities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>To find the cut: repeat <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times, keep the smallest.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2024B-p3"]}, "randomized/prob-basics": {"title": "Counting with Expectation & Quicksort", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>This is the foundation layer. The moment a problem asks \"how many <em>on average</em>\", \"expected running time\", or \"probability that <em>something</em> goes wrong\", you break the big messy quantity into tiny 0/1 pieces and add them up.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>\"Expected number of X\"</b> &rarr; write X as a sum of indicators, one per potential X, then use linearity.</li>\n<li><b>\"Probability at least one bad thing happens\"</b> &rarr; union bound.</li>\n<li><b>Quicksort / Quickselect runtime</b> &rarr; write a recurrence over the random pivot, say what the expectation is over, solve.</li>\n<li><b>\"Convert Monte Carlo &harr; Las Vegas\"</b> or \"ZPP/RP/BPP\" &rarr; cap the runtime, bound failure with Markov, amplify by repeating.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">LINEARITY OF EXPECTATION</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">always, even when the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are dependent — you never need independence here</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">INDICATOR TRICK</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nLet <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator for the event <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">counting problems: turn a count into a sum of yes/no events</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">UNION BOUND</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mo>&#x022C3;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">bounding the chance that ANY of several bad events occurs; no independence needed</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">MASTER THEOREM</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nFor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>c</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. E.g. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">solving divide-and-conquer recurrences</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gdef\"><span class=\"lab\">Definition</span> <b>ZPP</b> = Las Vegas (always correct, expected poly time). <b>RP</b> = one-sided error (rejects the \"no\" side sometimes). <b>BPP</b> = two-sided error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Key facts: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>2018B P4.</b> Randomized selection (find the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th smallest) — prove <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Whose randomness?</b> The analysis is over the <em>pivot coins</em>, not the input. So the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> bound holds for <em>every</em> worst-case input — state this first.</li>\n<li><b>Set up the recurrence.</b> A uniform pivot lands in each rank equally often, and we recurse on one side. Averaging over pivots gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Guess and verify.</b> Guess <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, plug into the sum, and check the constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> survives — that <em>is</em> the induction.</li>\n<li><b>Las Vegas &rarr; Monte Carlo.</b> Cap each run at, say, twice the expected time; by Markov it fails with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> capped trials: all fail with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> Do not assume independence to use linearity — you never need it. Conversely do not write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> unless independence is given. Always name what the expectation is taken over (coins vs. input). For Master Theorem, check <em>which</em> term dominates rather than pattern-matching.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Is the target an expected count, a runtime, or a failure probability?</li>\n<li>Count &rarr; one indicator per event, compute each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, sum by linearity.</li>\n<li>\"At least one\" &rarr; union bound over the bad events.</li>\n<li>Runtime &rarr; recurrence over the random choice; state the expectation's source; solve.</li>\n<li>Monte Carlo &harr; Las Vegas &rarr; cap, bound by Markov, amplify.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2018B-p4"]}, "randomized/probabilistic-method": {"title": "The Probabilistic Method — Existence by Averaging", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>You must prove that <em>some</em> object with a desired property <em>exists</em>, but you have no idea how to construct it. The trick: build a <em>random</em> object, compute the <em>expected</em> quality, and note that something must be at least as good as the average.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Prove there exists an assignment satisfying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> (fraction) of the clauses\" &rarr; random assignment + linearity.</li>\n<li>\"Show a coloring / partition / vector with property P exists\" &rarr; make it random, bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>quality</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, conclude.</li>\n<li>Want it <em>with high probability</em>, not just existence &rarr; add Chernoff + union bound.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core idea</h3>\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> <b>Averaging (first moment).</b> A random variable always attains a value at least its mean and one at most its mean:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>and</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nSo if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>, there <em>exists</em> an outcome with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math> (and one with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>). No construction needed.</div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">EXPECTED QUALITY VIA LINEARITY</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nWrite the quality as a sum of indicators (one per clause / edge / constraint):\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>satisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>clause&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;satisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">compute the average score of a random object piece by piece</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gdef\"><span class=\"lab\">Definition</span> <b>De-randomization (method of conditional expectations).</b> Once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>, fix the variables one at a time, each time keeping the conditional expectation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>: since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>p</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, at least one branch is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. This turns the existence proof into a deterministic algorithm.</div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>2017B P4.</b> Prove every 3-SAT formula with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> clauses has an assignment satisfying at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> clauses.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Randomize.</b> Set each variable to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Now the number satisfied, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, is a random variable.</li>\n<li><b>Score one clause.</b> A clause of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> literals fails only if all its literals are false: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>clause&#x000A0;satisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> (here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; for 3-literal clauses it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li><b>Sum by linearity.</b> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>clause&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;sat</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Dependence between clauses is irrelevant — linearity does not care.</li>\n<li><b>Invoke averaging.</b> Some assignment satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Done — and conditional expectations make it constructive.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> Averaging gives <em>existence of one</em> good outcome, not that a random object is good with high probability — for \"w.h.p.\" you need Chernoff + union bound. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math> only yields an object with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>; to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math> use the other side. Beware the true/false framing: \"at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> clauses\" is <em>false</em> because the average already guarantees <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, so a better-than-average assignment beats it.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Define a natural random object (uniform assignment / coloring / vector).</li>\n<li>Write the quality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> as a sum of indicators.</li>\n<li>Compute each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>piece&#x000A0;good</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> and add by linearity to get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Conclude an outcome with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> (or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>) exists.</li>\n<li>Optional: de-randomize by conditional expectations, or upgrade to w.h.p. with Chernoff.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["2017B-p4"]}, "randomized/random-walks": {"title": "Random Walks, Spectral Gap & Mixing", "html": "\n<h3>When do I reach for this?</h3>\n<p>You are moving randomly on a graph and want to know: will I reach a target? how fast do I forget where I started? The engine is <em>spectral</em> — the second eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> of the walk matrix controls how quickly the distribution flattens to uniform.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Does the walk reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within poly steps?\" &rarr; cover-time bound + Markov, or the mixing bound.</li>\n<li>\"How fast does it mix?\" &rarr; bound the spectral gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>\"Is the graph connected / bipartite?\" &rarr; read it off the spectrum (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff connected; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff bipartite).</li>\n<li>Space-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> connectivity (USTCON <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02208;</mo></mrow></math> RL) &rarr; a length-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> walk.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Core tools</h3>\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">NORMALIZED WALK OPERATOR</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>d</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> on edges (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-regular). Symmetric &amp; row-stochastic, so real eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> the top eigenvector. Lazy walk <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> forces all eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">setting up any walk analysis on a regular graph</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">MIXING BOUND</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nWith <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">\\|</mo><msup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">\\|</mo><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>t</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">bounding distance to uniform after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> steps; a small <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> means fast mixing</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gtool\"><div class=\"gtool-h\">COVER-TIME + MARKOV</div><div class=\"gtool-body\">\nCover time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; by Markov, from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> the walk visits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.\n<span class=\"gtool-use\">\"reach the target within poly steps w.h.p.\" with no spectral computation</span></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"gthm\"><span class=\"lab\">Theorem</span> <b>Spectral gap.</b> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is connected then the lazy walk has\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nSo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> is bounded away from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which forces mixing (and reachability) in polynomially many steps.</div>\n\n<h3>Worked example — the thinking</h3>\n<div class=\"gworked\">\n<p><b>hw2025s2 P1.</b> Prove the spectral-gap bound by exhibiting a large coordinate gap in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-eigenvector.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><b>Normalize.</b> Take a unit eigenvector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Then some coordinate has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Use orthogonality.</b> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mi>&#x027C2;</mi><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>k</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so some coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Now <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Walk the path.</b> Connectivity gives a path <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. By telescoping + Cauchy–Schwarz, some edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> on it has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><b>Convert to the gap.</b> Relate that edge difference to the Laplacian quadratic form to extract the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>d</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> factor. The <em>one</em> big edge difference is what a connected graph guarantees.</li>\n</ol>\n</div>\n\n<div class=\"box pitfall\"><b>Traps.</b> Use the <em>lazy</em> walk before invoking mixing — a bipartite graph has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the plain walk oscillates and never converges. Reachability needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> bounded away from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (connectivity), which is exactly the spectral-gap theorem. Do not confuse <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> distance with per-coordinate deviation; the mixing bound is on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</div>\n\n<h3>Checklist</h3>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Write the (lazy) normalized walk operator; note <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, eigenvector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Reachability fast? &rarr; cover-time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> + Markov, or the mixing bound.</li>\n<li>Mixing rate? &rarr; bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> via the spectral-gap argument (big coordinate gap along a path).</li>\n<li>Connectivity/bipartiteness &rarr; inspect <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>State the number of steps and the resulting success probability.</li>\n</ol>\n", "worked": ["hw2025s2-p1"]}};
